    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d951">

                <pb facs="1042" n="1030" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg1030"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.01/1075: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé at Algiers</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Chapin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-19T13:00:00+01:00">February 19,
                            1944—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>537. From Reinhardt. Reber reports that last night a circular, numbered 1, from
                    the Executive Junta was being circulated in Naples and reached several American
                    and British correspondents. It was addressed to all employees and officials of
                    the State, administrations and organizations, to be distributed by the
                    provincial Committees of National Liberation and contained the allegation that
                    because he failed to abdicate in response to the request of the Bari Congress
                    the King has rebelled against the nation. The circular calls upon all officials
                    and personnel of all state organizations, administrations and bodies both
                    military and civil, “not to participate in rebellion of the King and his
                    accomplices”. It was clear that the circular constitutes an incitement to civil
                    disobedience or even revolt although it did not [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >sic</hi>] say that all persons should remain at their posts in the service
                    of the nation which has now resumed the exercise of its sovereign powers.</p>
                <p>In view of the foregoing and with the approval of the CinC<anchor n="61"
                        xml:id="d951-f1"/> the Allied Control Commission imposed a complete
                    censorship stoppage on military grounds. It appears however that AP
                    correspondent had already filed a story based on the circular which may have
                    been stopped. The AP office has protested against this censorship but it should
                    be understood that the censorship was imposed not on political grounds but for
                    reasons of military security.</p>
                <pb facs="1043" n="1031" xml:id="pg1031"/>
                <p>The CinC has informed the Junta that he cannot permit such encouragements to
                    disorder.</p>
                <p>A further report on the situation will be submitted later. [Reinhardt.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chapin</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
